A||||6||duke||Oil bypass||||5||
Z||000000||duke||11-05-2001||07:09 PM||||Does anyone know how to check the oil bypass valve on a Duke? I was told it was where the oil filter screws onto the block. I have a low oil pressure problem and was told this may be stuck? Is it something I can see by removing the filter?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-05-2001||07:23 PM||||acording to the info I have, the bypass for stock Fiero motors using spin on filters is in/under the threaded fitting the filter screws onto. You have to remove the filter and the fitting which won't be fun.<P>Last I saw.... the valve is available in the AC delco and/or Fram catalogs. Fram's online catalog seems to be down right now so I can't check.<P>It doesn't hurt to check the vavle but that's probably not your proble. More likely a wear issue in the pump, engine, or both.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||11-05-2001||09:11 PM||||The bypass valve can be seen by removing the filter.  It is pressed into the block next to the threaded pipe that the filter screws onto.  You should see a metal ring with a flat metal disc in the middle.  This disc is the valve and it should be closed.  I doubt that this valve could affect the oil pressure since its purpose is to bypass the filter in case of a filter clog.  When Pontiac replaced the engine in my 84, they neglected to install this valve.  I didn't realize it until I tore the engine down for a rebuild about 180,000 miles later!  The engine had no signs of oil pressure problems at all.  <P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||duke||11-06-2001||02:28 PM||||Thanks for the feedback. The motor was just rebuilt including new bearings. There is just a trickle of oil coming out of the side of the block where the oil pressure gauge connects. The oil pump was replaced but it's still the same. Someone suggested the bypass valve, but I too thought that the pressure shoule still be high even if this was stuck open. Any other thoughts?||||reg||5
Z||000004||DaveL||11-06-2001||03:48 PM||||During the rebuild, did any one clean out all the oil passages in the block? Or maybe the screen on the oil pump is clogged with crunge....<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000005||theogre||11-06-2001||07:15 PM||||Could be the new pump is bad or not installed right. If the pickup tube got whacked it may be sucking air.<P>could also be the pressure regulator that's usually on the pump stuck open.||||reg||1
Z||000006||duke||11-06-2001||10:15 PM||||The block was completely hot tanked to get out all the sludge and grime prior to the rebuild so I would say that the oil passages were cleaned out. I have tried two different oil pumps and the problem is still the same. I changed the pump, pickup tube, and screen. I am all out of ideas as to what it could be. I also took compressed air and blew it into the hole that the oil pressure gauge connects to on the side of the block to make sure there was no blockage. I really don't want to tear the motor back apart, but unless I find the problem, I guess I will have to.||||reg||1
